
Create a regression test for the nearest stars list. Ensure the default Stellar Neighborhood matches this list: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_nearest_stars ignoring the differing distance limits; Stellar Neighborhood may list stars beyond 20 l.y. by default. Flag any out-of-sequence stars, any stars in the Wikipedia list missing from Stellar Neighborhood, and any extra stars in Stellar Neighborhood closer than 20 l.y. that are not in the Wikipedia list or are physically located farther away than Stellar Neighborhood indicates.

Create a regression test that all names, identifiers, and searchable text use only the characters directly typable on an en-US keyboard, exempting exoname.dat names and Bayer Greek letters. These characters are A-Z, a-z, 0-9, `~!@#$%^&*()-_=+[{\|}];:'",<.>/? and space. All exonames that use accented characters are searchable by their plain equivalents, e.g. AEgir, Ditso. Additionally, keyboard shortcuts are limited to these characters, the function keys, and their shifted, ctrl'd, and/or alt'd variants.

Accept all unconfirmed exoplanets, as we currently do, from NASA and EXOplanet.eu, and accept all controversial planets. Continue excluding refuted planets, as we already do. Assign one of the function keys, and create a menu item, to turn on and off the display of unconfirmed and controversial planets as a single category, and any objects orbiting them.

Audit the dot sizes and flare sizes of bright objects. Compare the Sun and Moon from Earth, Venus from Earth, all eight planets from the Sun, the Sun from all eight planets, and Sirius, Vega, Betelgeuse, Deneb, Epsilon Eridani, Alcyone, and TX Piscium from Earth, and evaluate the accuracy, both in pretty-colors and realistic colors, of each object's displayed pixels compared to its real-world apparent brightness. The Sun and Moon have discs so disc pixels contribute to display brightness; measure the Moon at full phase. Determine whether flares are too big for bright objects and whether mathematical adjustments are warranted in the code for dots and/or flares, keeping the current maximum flare size limit intact.

